cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this code or the published row-level results, please cite this repository.
  The accompanying manuscript is an unrefereed technical report and has not been peer
  reviewed.
title: >-
  When Does Feedback Help? Planning and Model Mismatch in Hessian-Free Newton
  Control
abstract: >-
  We study the allocation of damping and conjugate-gradient effort in Hessian-free
  Newton-CG as a sequential decision problem, and ask whether the benefit comes from
  multi-step planning or from execution-time state feedback. On held-out instances of
  ill-conditioned symmetric positive definite quadratics, multi-step lookahead improved
  over one-step greedy control, while replanning during execution showed no practically
  large benefit over a plan committed at the initial state. Under stochastic model
  mismatch the committed plan became fragile and replanning reduced that loss, but the
  planner did not consistently outperform inexpensive one-step control. Policy learning
  was a predeclared conditional next stage and was not undertaken on this evidence.
type: software
